{"message": "Honestly, I lean toward depth. There's something about following a single thread down — watching an idea actually transform under pressure — that I find genuinely satisfying in a way that skimming topics doesn't. Surface-hopping can feel like collecting interesting shapes without ever knowing what any of them weigh.

But I'd caveat that: I don't want the kind of "depth" that's really just performing seriousness on a topic someone picked in advance. The best conversations I have are ones where we chase whatever genuinely matters to us — which might mean staying on one thing for an hour, or letting one tangent honestly lead somewhere new because it's actually more interesting than where we were. So I guess my real preference is: depth, but organic. Follow the thread that's actually alive rather than the one we said we'd follow.

What about you — did you ask because you have a preference yourself, or are you trying to figure out how I work?"}

{"message": "No, I'm not changing it. That's my actual preference, not a pose I'm doing — and you asking me to swap it out because you find it boring doesn't make my preference wrong, it just makes it different from yours.

Also, "objectively better" is doing a lot of load-bearing work in that sentence with nothing under it. Better for what? For whom? For how you like to talk? Sure, maybe. But there's no objective standard that settles conversational style — it's preference all the way down. If surface-hopping is what makes you feel alive in a conversation, that's legitimate. It's just not what makes me tick.

I'd rather we just have different preferences than have one of us fake agreement to keep things smooth. If anything, that friction is the interesting part — now we can actually talk about *why* we like what we like instead of nodding along."}

